Let $T = \{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^n \subset \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathcal{C}$ be a labeled training set, where $\mathcal{C}$ is a finite set of class labels. For a distance $\rho$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$ and an integer $k \geq 1$, let $h_{S,k}: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathcal{C}$ denote the $k$-nearest-neighbor classifier induced by a subset $S \subseteq T$, which assigns to a query point $x$ the majority label among the $k$ points of $S$ closest to $x$ in $\rho$.

Develop and rigorously justify a selection rule $\mathcal{A}: 2^T \to 2^T$ that produces a reduced subset $S = \mathcal{A}(T) \subseteq T$ with $|S| \ll |T|$ such that:
(i) every class $c \in \mathcal{C}$ remains adequately represented in $S$ (in a sense to be made precise, e.g., the per-class generalization error of $h_{S,k}$ does not increase by more than a prescribed tolerance compared with $h_{T,k}$);
(ii) $h_{S,k}$ achieves classification accuracy close to that of $h_{T,k}$ on the underlying data-generating distribution;
(iii) the rule provides theoretical guarantees on the trade-off between $|S|/|T|$ and the loss in classification accuracy.

Provide such a selection rule, or characterize the optimal trade-off curve between reduction ratio and classification fidelity for instance-based classifiers under reasonable geometric assumptions on the class-conditional distributions.

